Here's a suggested API design for a 3D camera animation service in TypeScript:

```typescript
// CameraAnimationService.ts
import { Injectable } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class CameraAnimationService {
  async animateCamera(scene: any, camera: any, animationData: any) {
    // Validate input data
    if (!scene || !camera || !animationData) {
      throw new Error('Invalid input data');
    }

    // Initialize the camera animation
    const animation = scene.animate({
      duration: animationData.duration,
      easingFunction: animationData.easingFunction,
      properties: {
        position: animationData.position,
        rotation: animationData.rotation,
        scale: animationData.scale,
      },
    });

    // Start the camera animation
    await animation.start();
  }
}
```

To use this API, you can create a controller that handles the incoming requests and calls the `animateCamera` method on the `CameraAnimationService` instance. Here's an example of how you might do this in a NestJS application:

```typescript
// camera-animation.controller.ts
import { Controller, Post } from '@nestjs/common';
import { CameraAnimationService } from './camera-animation.service';

@Controller('camera-animation')
export class CameraAnimationController {
  constructor(private readonly cameraAnimationService: CameraAnimationService) {}

  @Post()
  async animateCamera(scene: any, camera: any, animationData: any) {
    await this.cameraAnimationService.animateCamera(scene, camera, animationData);
  }
}
```

In this example, the `animateCamera` method is called with the scene, camera, and animation data as parameters. The method validates the input data, initializes the camera animation, and starts the animation.

