Here's a simple SystemVerilog module for an LDPC (Low-Density Parity-Check) encoder for FPGA implementation:

```verilog
module ldpc_encoder(
    input wire clk,
    input wire reset,
    input wire data_in[],
    output wire data_out[]
);

reg [7:0] state;
reg [7:0] coeff[];

always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
begin
    if (reset)
    begin
        state <= 8'b00000000;
        coeff <= 8'b11111111;
    end
    else
    begin
        state <= state + 1;
        coeff <= coeff - 1;
    end
end

always @*
begin
    for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++)
    {
        data_out[i] <= (data_in[i] & coeff[i]) ^ (state[i] & ~coeff[i]);
    }
end

endmodule
```

This module implements a simple LDPC encoder with a fixed coefficient matrix and a state machine to control the encoding process. The `data_in` array represents the input data, and the `data_out` array represents the output encoded data. The `clk` and `reset` signals are used to control the timing and initialization of the module.

Note that this is a simplified example and may not be suitable for all FPGA applications. For more complex LDPC encoders, you may need to use more advanced techniques such as parallel processing, pipelining, or memory optimization.

