To use a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator, you can add a few lines of code to your existing MATLAB script. Here's the modified code:

```matlab
% Load input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');

% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% Apply Gaussian filter
filtered_image = imfilter(gray_image, fspecial('gaussian', [5 5], 0.5));

% Define Sobel operator in x direction
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];

% Define Sobel operator in y direction
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');

% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');

% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);

% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```

In this modified code, we first apply a Gaussian filter to the grayscale image using the `imfilter` function. The `fspecial` function is used to create a Gaussian kernel with a standard deviation of 0.5. The `imfilter` function applies the kernel to the image and returns a filtered image.

After applying the Gaussian filter, we proceed with the Sobel edge detection as before. We define the Sobel operators in the x and y directions, apply them to the filtered image using `conv2`, and combine the results to get the final edge image.

The resulting edge image will have fewer artifacts and be more accurate than the original edge image without the Gaussian filter.

