text1 = fileread('Text1.txt'); %read text1 from file
text2 = fileread('Text2.txt'); %read text2 from file
%Convert text to lowercase and split into words
words1 = lower(strsplit(text1));
words2 = lower(strsplit(text2));
%Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
uniqueWords = setdiff(words1, words2);
%Print the unique words
disp(uniqueWords);

response:
Your implementation is correct and efficient for finding unique words in Text1 that do not appear in Text2. It uses setdiff function which is designed for fast set operations, making it suitable for handling large amounts of text.

To further optimize the performance, you can consider using parallel processing with the parpool function. This allows you to distribute the workload across multiple cores or processors, thus reducing the execution time for large datasets. Here's an example of how to use parpool with your existing code:

```matlab
text1 = fileread('Text1.txt'); %read text1 from file
text2 = fileread('Text2.txt'); %read text2 from file
%Convert text to lowercase and split into words
words1 = lower(strsplit(text1));
words2 = lower(strsplit(text2));
%Create a parallel pool with 4 workers (adjust this value according to your machine's resources)
pp = parpool('local', 4);
%Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
uniqueWords = setdiff(words1, words2);
%Close the parallel pool
close(pp);
%Print the unique words
disp(uniqueWords);
```

In this example, we create a parallel pool with 4 workers using the parpool function. The 'local' option specifies that the pool should be created on the local machine. You can adjust the number of workers based on your machine's resources. After creating the pool, we perform the same operation as before, but now it will be executed in parallel across all workers. Finally, we close the parallel pool using the close function.

Remember to test your code on different datasets to ensure that it performs well and does not cause any memory issues.

