3. Use a loop to iterate through each character in the input string and apply the appropriate Morse Code representation.
4. Join the resulting Morse Code characters into a single string and return it.

Here's an example of how you can implement this approach in MATLAB:
```matlab
% Define a cell array containing the Morse Code representations for each character
morse_code = {'.-', '-...', '-.-.', '-..', '.', '..-', '--.', '....', '..', '.---', '-.', '.-..', '--', '-.', '---', '.--.', '--.-', '.-', '...', '-', '..-', '...-', '.--', '-..-', '-.--', '--..'};

% Create a function that takes a string as input and returns the corresponding Morse Code representation
function morse_representation = convert_to_morse(input_string)
    % Convert the input string to uppercase
    input_string = strupper(input_string);

    % Initialize an empty string to store the Morse Code representation
    morse_representation = '';

    % Loop through each character in the input string and apply the appropriate Morse Code representation
    for i = 1:length(input_string)
        % Get the current character
        current_char = input_string(i);

        % Check if the current character is a letter or a digit
        if isletter(current_char)
            % Get the Morse Code representation for the current character
            morse_representation = [morse_representation, morse_code{strfind(morse_code, current_char)}];
        elseif isdigit(current_char)
            % Get the Morse Code representation for the current character
