The Floating-Point Unit (FPU) is a specialized hardware component integrated into the Cortex-M4F processor of the STM32F407VG microcontroller. Its primary function is to perform arithmetic operations on floating-point numbers, which are essential for various applications such as scientific computing, financial calculations, and control systems.

How it Works:
The FPU operates using a combination of hardware and software components. At the hardware level, the FPU consists of a set of registers, including the floating-point register file, status register, and control register. These registers are used to store and manipulate floating-point numbers, as well as to control the operation of the FPU.

At the software level, the FPU is controlled by a set of instructions known as floating-point instructions. These instructions allow developers to perform a variety of operations on floating-point numbers, such as addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, and square root. The FPU also supports a range of advanced features, such as denormals, infinities, and NaNs (Not a Number).

How to Use it:
