Yes, you can adjust the layout of widgets in the WP-Admin area by using the built-in WordPress customizer or by adding custom CSS.

To use the customizer:

1. Log in to your WordPress admin dashboard.
2. Go to "Appearance" > "Customize."
3. Click on the "Widgets" section.
4. Here, you can add, remove, and rearrange widgets in the desired order. You can also resize and adjust the width of widget areas.
5. Once you're satisfied with the layout, click on the "Publish" button to save your changes.

Alternatively, you can add custom CSS to adjust the layout of widgets. To do this:

1. Log in to your WordPress admin dashboard.
2. Go to "Appearance" > "Editor."
3. Select the theme file where you want to add the custom CSS (usually style.css).
4. Add the following code at the bottom of the file:
```css
/* Adjust widget layout */
.widget {
    width: 100%; /* Set the widget width to 100% to make them full-width */
}

.widget-area {
    flex-basis: 33.33%; /* Set the widget area width to 33.33% for a three-column layout */
}

@media only screen and (max-width: 768px) {
    .widget-area {
        flex-basis: 100%; /* Set the widget area width to 100% for smaller screens */
    }
}
```
1. Save the file.

This code will set the widget width to 100% and the widget area width to 33.33% for a three-column layout. You can adjust these values as needed to create the desired layout. The media query ensures that the layout is optimized for smaller screens.

Remember to replace the existing code in the style.css file with the above code, or add it at the end of the file if there is already some custom CSS.

SQL is a language used for managing relational databases and it doesn't have built-in functionality for creating reminders or prompting users. However, you can use SQL to schedule a job that will check the database for the reminder condition and execute a command to display the break reminder message.

To achieve this, you'll need to use a database management system (DBMS) that supports scheduling jobs, such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, or Microsoft SQL Server. Here's an example using MySQL:

1. Create a stored procedure to check if 10 minutes have passed since the start_time of the current work session:
```sql
CREATE PROCEDURE `check_for_break`()
BEGIN
  DECLARE current_work_session INT;
  SELECT id INTO current_work_session FROM work_sessions ORDER BY start_time DESC LIMIT 1;

  IF current_work_session IS NOT NULL THEN
    SET current_timestamp = NOW();
    SET break_due_at = current_timestamp + INTERVAL 10 MINUTE;

    IF current_timestamp > break_due_at THEN
      -- Display break reminder message
      SELECT 'It's time for a break!' AS message;
    END IF;
  END IF;
END;
```
1. Schedule the stored procedure to run every minute:
```sql
CREATE EVENT `check_for_break_event`
ON SCHEDULE EVERY 1 MINUTE
DO CALL check_for_break();
```
This event will call the `check_for_break` procedure every minute, checking if it's time for a break based on the current work session's start time. If 10 minutes have passed since the start_time, the procedure will display the break reminder message.

Note that this solution assumes that you have a way to display messages to the user outside of the database, such as a web application or a desktop client. The specific method for displaying the break reminder message will depend on the tool or framework you're using to interact with the database.

