It seems like the issue is with the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header being set to `*` or a wildcard, which allows any origin to access the resource. This might be preventing you from including the authorization header with the request.

To resolve this issue, you can try setting the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to a specific domain or a list of domains that you trust. For example:

```
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://example.com
```

or

```
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://example.com, https://example2.com
```

This will allow requests from the specified domains only, and you should be able to include the authorization header with the request.

Alternatively, you can use a more fine-grained approach and set the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to `*` with a preflight request. This will allow the client to send a preflight request to the server to check if the request is allowed. If the preflight request is successful, the client can then send the actual request with the authorization header.

Here's an example of how you can set up a preflight request:

1. Add the following code to your server:

```python
from flask import Flask, request, jsonify

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/preflight', methods=['OPTIONS'])
def preflight():
    headers = request.headers
    if 'Authorization' in headers:
        return jsonify({'status': 'success', 'message': 'Authorization header allowed'})
    else:
        return jsonify({'status': 'error', 'message': 'Authorization header not allowed'})

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(debug=True)
```

2. Send a preflight request to the server using the following code:

```javascript
fetch('https://example.com/preflight', {
  method: 'OPTIONS',
  headers: {
    'Authorization': 'Bearer your_authorization_token'
  }
});
```

