A logical syllogism is a form of argument consisting of two premises and a conclusion that follows logically from the premises. Here's an example of a logical syllogism:

Premise 1: All humans are mortal.
Premise 2: Socrates is human.
Conclusion: Socrates is mortal.

This syllogism works by using the first premise to establish a general rule about all humans, and then using the second premise to identify Socrates as a specific instance of a human. The conclusion then follows logically from the premises, stating that Socrates must be mortal because all humans are mortal.

Here's the same syllogism formatted in LaTeX:

\begin{syllogism}
    \premise{All humans are mortal.}
    \premise{Socrates is human.}
    \conclusion{Socrates is mortal.}
\end{syllogism}

