Here's an example of how you can use the Accord.NET library to train and implement the Naive Bayes algorithm for text classification:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;
using Accord.MachineLearning.Text;
using Accord.Math.Statistics;

namespace TextClassification
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Load the dataset
            var dataset = new[]
            {
                new { Label = "Sports", Sentence = "The sports game was exciting." },
                new { Label = "Politics", Sentence = "The political candidate made a strong speech." },
                new { Label = "Entertainment", Sentence = "The movie was fantastic." }
            };

            // Preprocess the data
            var preprocessor = new TextPreprocessing.Tokenizer(new[] { ' ' });
            var tokens = dataset.SelectMany(d => d.Sentence.Split(' ')).ToArray();
            var vocabulary = new HashSet<string>(tokens);

            // Create a Naive Bayes classifier
            var naiveBayes = new NaiveBayes.MultinomialNaiveBayes();
            naiveBayes.Fit(tokens, dataset.Select(d => d.Label));

            // Classify a new sentence
            var newSentence = "The sports game was exciting.";
            var classifiedLabel = naiveBayes.Predict(preprocessor.Transform(newSentence).Select(t => vocabulary.Contains(t) ? t : "<UNK>"));
            Console.WriteLine($"Predicted label: {classifiedLabel}");
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we first load the dataset and preprocess the data by tokenizing the sentences and creating a vocabulary. We then create a Naive Bayes classifier using the MultinomialNaiveBayes algorithm and fit it to the training data. Finally, we classify a new sentence using the Predict method and print the predicted label.

