To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can implement a simple check for proximity before adding a new planet. If the new planet is too close to an existing planet, you can reject it and generate a new one until it meets the distance criteria.

Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

This updated function checks if the new planet is too close to any existing planet using the `some()` method. If it is, it rejects the planet and generates a new one until it meets the distance criteria. The `attempts` variable keeps track of the number of attempts made to generate a valid planet, and the function returns after either `numPlanets` planets have been generated or 100 attempts have been made.

You can adjust the `minDistance` parameter to control the maximum distance between planets. A smaller value will result in closer planets, while a larger value will result in more spaced-out planets.

With this modification, your planets should be distributed more evenly and avoid clumping together.

