To make the planets less crowded, you can introduce a minimum distance between each planet when generating them. You can modify your `generateRandomPlanets` function to check for collisions before adding a new planet. If there's a collision, you can generate another random position until you find one that doesn't collide with any other planet. Here's an updated version of the function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

Make sure to pass the desired minimum distance to the function when calling it. For example:

```javascript
setPlanets(generateRandomPlanets(5, 35));
```

This will ensure that the generated planets are not too close together, providing a better visual experience.

Here's a simple React utility to remove widows. This utility will check if a paragraph has a single word on the last line and remove it if it does.

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidow = ({ children }) => {
  const [paragraph, setParagraph] = useState(children);

  const handleRemoveWidow = () => {
    const words = paragraph.split(' ');
    const lastWordIndex = words.length - 1;

    if (lastWordIndex >= 0 && words[lastWordIndex].trim() === '') {
      words.splice(lastWordIndex, 1);
      setParagraph(words.join(' '));
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <p>{paragraph}</p>
      <button onClick={handleRemoveWidow}>Remove Widow</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidow;
```

